# surface — what a hundred years outdoors does to the stone.
#
# This fragment holds almost no geometry. What it holds is WEATHER: the statements that make a
# stone building read as a stone building that has stood in the rain rather than as a drawing of
# one delivered this morning. Sand and dust at the foot of the steps and in the hollows of the
# mouldings; the sun bleaching the treads while the risers stay clean; the sharp arrises of a
# cornice worn round while the hollow behind them keeps its edge; moss and lichen on the north
# side, where the wall never dries.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS FILE PUTS UNDER LOAD: the weathering subsystem, and the three field operations that
# exist only to serve it.
#
#   Weather::Desert       curvature-driven scour, and two coats keyed on facing and on height
#   Weather::Overgrown    occlusion-driven growth, and two coats keyed on cavity and facing
#   Op::Curvature         seven evaluations of the whole building per point. Desert's only input.
#   Op::Occlusion         fourteen. Overgrown's only input.
#   Op::Facing            six, through normal_at. All four coats read it.
#   WeatherRequest::scope the `on=` half — the reason the last facility shipped with no weathering
#                         at all, and the only part of this that is new
#
# Nothing else in the building weathers except one hairline coat on the site's boundary plinth
# (`site.clip`, `weather cracks 0.10 on=site_weather_zone`), and cracks reads none of the three
# operations above. Delete the two `weather` statements at the bottom of this file and
# `Op::Curvature`, `Op::Occlusion` and `Op::Facing` are dead code again, along with
# `WeatherRequest::scope`, and four of the five entries in `enum class Weather` have never been
# run on anything. That is why this file exists and it is the whole of what it does.
# DO NOT DELETE THE WEATHER STATEMENTS BECAUSE THE FILE LOOKS EMPTY.
#
# It is also the only place in the building where a material is invented rather than declared:
# `apply_weather` calls `make_material` for `sand`, `bleached`, `moss` and `lichen`, none of which
# is in `_contract.clip`. So this fragment adds four materials to the palette that nothing else
# uses — four more rows in the voxel type table and four more BSDFs in a frame.
#
# READ clips/facility/requests/surface.md BEFORE SWITCHING THIS ON, BECAUSE IT IS NOT FREE.
#
# Every coat these two kinds add is unsettleable, so it is asked once per solid voxel of the WHOLE
# clip, and each ask is twenty evaluations of the whole building's distance field. Measured: one
# line costs a small clip 12x its sample time and the two together 114x; on real facility geometry
# — site, podium, steps and walls at metre 8 — the pair costs 362 ms -> 237,303 ms, which is 656x.
# The complete building with these two lines in it did not finish at metre 5 in forty-five minutes.
#
# That is a property of the two KINDS and not of the scope. `on=` is free, and it is the half of
# this that works exactly as advertised: measured against a control with the same weather lines
# and their zones moved into empty air, not one voxel of lawn, water or bronze changed and all
# 93,950 weathered voxels came out of stone. The request file has the numbers, and the two engine
# changes that would make the rest of it affordable.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE MODULE. Every box below is a multiple or a simple fraction of M = 0.45, with three named
# exceptions, all of them heights taken from the elevation rather than invented: 2.40 is the
# walls' first rustication joint (podium_top plus one 0.60 course), 11.475 is 25.5 M and 5.40 and
# 5.85 are 12 M and 13 M — see each zone.
#
# WHERE THE ZONES ARE AND WHY. The building faces south, at negative z. So:
#
#   the south and the top   sun, wind and dust. Desert.
#   the north              shade and damp. Overgrown.
#
# A building weathers by which way it faces, so the fragment that weathers it is organised the
# same way. And every boundary of every zone is put on an arris the elevation already has — a
# bed mould's springing, a rustication joint, the top of a string course — because the mask has a
# soft edge about 0.05 m wide and an edge that lands in the middle of a flat face reads as a line
# somebody drew. `site.clip` learned this first and its note is worth reading.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART — 0.225 m of granite that nobody will ever see
# ================================================================================================
#
# The manifest's unions want a `part_<name>` from every fragment, and this fragment's output is
# two statements rather than a shape. So this is a setting-out stone: a half-module cube buried in
# the middle of the podium's mass on the centre line, granite on all six sides of it, adding
# nothing to any surface in the building.
#
# Buried rather than merely small, on purpose. Anything on a surface would be one more object the
# next person has to design around, and this fragment's whole claim is that it changes no
# geometry: it changes what the geometry is made of.
let surface_datum = box -0.1125 0.5625 -2.3625   0.1125 0.7875 -2.1375 round=0.015

let part_surface = surface_datum

# This fragment hollows nothing. Said out loud, as the template asks, so the manifest never has to
# wonder whether a fragment carved something:
# there is no void_surface.

# Granite, because it is inside the podium's granite and a voxel of it that somehow reached air
# should not announce itself. below=0.02 like every other rule in the building: the manifest
# displaces everything by 12 mm of grain at the end, and a test at zero misses the voxels the
# grain made.
paint granite where=surface_datum below=0.02

# ================================================================================================
# THE ZONES — boxes, and the reason they are boxes
# ================================================================================================
#
# `weather ... on=<shape>` evaluates the named shape once per voxel of the whole clip, because the
# scope compiles into every coat's test and `metric_slack(Op::Smoothstep)` is infinite, so the
# sampler can never settle it for a block. The site fragment measured 1,453 ms for a four-box
# scope against 574,333 ms for a scope naming its own stonework — the same coat over the same
# stone, two hundred and eleven times the cost.
#
# So every zone here is a box, or a difference of two boxes, and never `on=part_walls`.
#
# They are also written so that nothing can get them wrong by being generous. A weathering coat is
# appended AFTER every fragment's paint and would sand a bronze urn or bleach a hedge as readily
# as it dusts a step, so each zone is checked against its neighbours and the check is written down.

# --- the sunny side: the great steps and the podium face they climb to -------------------------
#
# x to +-9.90 (22 M): the flight is +-9.45 and this is a module beyond its cheek walls.
# z from -15.75 (35 M) to -12.60 (28 M): the bottom nosing, to a module INSIDE the podium's south
# face at -12.825, so the far edge of the mask is buried in stone and cannot show.
# y from -0.45 (-M) to 1.80 (podium_top): the terrace is the ceiling, which is a real arris.
#
# The floor is below the ground on purpose, so that the gravel forecourt takes the drift at the
# foot of the flight, which is where sand actually ends up. The zone is gravel and not lawn:
# site.clip paints the whole southern approach gravel from the boundary out to z = -13.05. The two
# reflecting basins start at x = 11.70 and the site's bronze urns stand at x = +-10.575, both
# well outside +-9.90.
let surface_sun_steps = box -9.90 -0.45 -15.75   9.90 1.80 -12.60

# --- the sunny side: the wash of the main cornice ----------------------------------------------
#
# The corona and the crowning cyma at the top of the entablature — the one band of the elevation
# that the rain reaches and the sun stands on all day, which is what a mason calls the wash.
#
# A RING and not a box: 0.675 m (1.5 M) wide round the outside of the building, y from 11.475
# (25.5 M, just under the corona's bottom arris at 11.52) to 12.15 (27 M, air).
#
# The ring is what keeps this off everything it must not touch, and every number in it is a
# clearance:
#   the lead roof deck    x +-15.85, z +-7.35   -> the inner box at +-16.20 and 7.65 excludes it
#   the parapet and dies  out to about x 16.00  -> same
#   the parapet's urns    y 12.25 and above     -> under the 12.15 ceiling
#   the pediment corners  y 11.85 to 12.35 at z -12.1  -> inside the inner box in z, excluded
#   the entablature's bronze swags and paterae, on the frieze at 10.55 to 11.15 -> below 11.475
let surface_wash_out = box -16.875 11.475 -13.950   16.875 12.150  8.550
let surface_wash_in  = box -16.200 11.250 -13.050   16.200 12.600  7.650
let surface_wash     = difference { surface_wash_out surface_wash_in }

let surface_sun = union { surface_sun_steps surface_wash }

# --- the damp side: the foot of the north wall -------------------------------------------------
#
# North is +z and the north wall is the one that never dries. This box takes the podium's north
# bed mould and corona and the first rusticated course of the wall above it.
#
# y from 1.35 (3 M) — the springing of the podium's bed mould, a real edge — to 2.40, which is the
# walls' first horizontal rustication joint. 2.40 is not a module multiple and it is not invented
# either: it is podium_top plus one 0.60 course, and it is the one line the elevation already has
# at that height. The soft edge of the mask lands in a 0.09 m channel and disappears. It also
# clears the ground-storey window sills, which start at 2.52, so no glass and no glazing bar is
# in this zone.
#
# z from 6.75 (15 M) to 8.55 (19 M). The wall's outer face is at 7.50 and its INNER face at 6.60,
# so the box lies entirely within the thickness of the wall and outward of it: nothing inside the
# building is in it. That matters — moss belongs on a wall that can see the sky, and the rotunda
# is two metres further in. The site's north hedge is below y = 0.27 and nowhere near.
let surface_damp_foot = box -16.875 1.35 6.75   16.875 2.40 8.55

# --- the damp side: the north string course ----------------------------------------------------
#
# The one projecting ledge on the north elevation, and the best thing in the building for this: a
# bold corona with a flat soffit and an ovolo weathering above it, standing out to z = 7.68. An
# up-facing ledge on a north wall is where growth starts and the hollow under its soffit is where
# it stays.
#
# y 5.40 to 5.85 is 12 M to 13 M AND is exactly the corona's bottom arris and the ovolo's top, so
# both edges of the mask land on edges that are carved. The piano nobile's sills are at 6.12 and
# the ground storey's arched keystones top out at 5.40, so the zone touches stone and nothing else.
let surface_damp_ledge = box -16.875 5.40 7.20   16.875 5.85 8.10

let surface_damp = union { surface_damp_foot surface_damp_ledge }

# ================================================================================================
# THE WEATHER
# ================================================================================================
#
# DESERT FIRST AND OVERGROWN SECOND, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLISTIC ORDERING. `apply_weather` re-reads
# `script.solid` at the top of every request and every kind ends by displacing it, so the second
# request occludes a field the first has already deepened. Written this way round the pair costs
# 47.0 s on the bench clip; written the other way round, for identical output, it costs 84.8 s.
# 1.8x, for swapping two lines. Do not swap them.
#
# LOW AMOUNTS. This is a public building that is looked after, not a ruin.
#
#   desert 0.18     The scour displaces at most 0.05 x scale x amount = 9 mm, and only where the
#                   curvature says arris — about a third of a voxel at metre 32, which softens a
#                   nosing without eating it. `level = -0.45` is the datum the sand drifts up from,
#                   set a module BELOW the lawn so the drift dies out about a metre up the flight
#                   instead of covering it: the height term is one at the level and zero 1.5 x
#                   scale above it. The bleach coat's threshold is 0.55 - 0.35a = 0.487 against a
#                   test of 0.6 x facing + 0.4 x grain, so it lands on up-facing surfaces where
#                   the grain is over about -0.28 and NEVER on a vertical one, where the test
#                   cannot reach 0.4 however long it stands there. Treads bleach, risers stay
#                   clean. That is what rain does and it is the one part of this that is free.
#
#   overgrown 0.18  Swells the surface by at most 0.045 x scale x amount = 7 mm where growth takes
#                   hold. Lichen's threshold is 0.55 - 0.35a = 0.487 and moss's is 0.85 - 0.55a =
#                   0.751, against 0.55 x cavity + 0.35 x facing + 0.4 x clumps. A flat exposed
#                   wall face has a cavity of about 0.5 and a facing of zero, so it reaches
#                   0.275 + clumps: patchy lichen, and no moss at all, ever, at any amount below
#                   about 0.9. A re-entrant corner with an up-facing ledge in it reaches 0.76
#                   before the clumps are counted, so the moss goes exactly where the ledge meets
#                   the wall and the soffit meets the corona. That is the whole argument for
#                   driving this off occlusion instead of off a texture, and on the bench clip it
#                   is the difference between a green band along a cornice and green paint.
#
# The four materials these lines invent — sand 198,176,132; bleached 208,202,188; moss 74,108,54;
# lichen 138,148,108 — are made by `make_material` at the colours written into clip_script.cpp,
# because _contract.clip does not declare them and this fragment deliberately does not either.
# Four names belonging to the whole building should not be claimed by one fragment; the request
# file asks for them to be added to the contract instead.

weather desert    0.18 scale=1.00 level=-0.45 seed=83 on=surface_sun
weather overgrown 0.18 scale=0.90 seed=89 on=surface_damp
